issue_context.body:
Hello, the SRC folks asked that I file this publicly:

In the client-go repo, util/cert/cert.go , function
`GenerateSelfSignedCertKeyWithFixtures()` creates a private key and stores
it into a file with mode `0644`:

https://github.com/kubernetes/client-go/blob/acab036eff5706db06b4eb79253165cf4be0d9c5/util/cert/cert.go#L184

```
        // Generate key
        keyBuffer := bytes.Buffer{}
        if err := pem.Encode(&keyBuffer, &pem.Block{Type: keyutil.RSAPrivateKeyBlockType, Bytes: x509.MarshalPKCS1PrivateKey(priv)}); err != nil {
                return nil, nil, err
        }
         
        if len(fixtureDirectory) > 0 {
                if err := ioutil.WriteFile(certFixturePath, certBuffer.Bytes(), 0644); err != nil {
                        return nil, nil, fmt.Errorf("failed to write cert fixture to %s: %v", certFixturePath, err)
                }
                if err := ioutil.WriteFile(keyFixturePath, keyBuffer.Bytes(), 0644); err != nil {
                        return nil, nil, fmt.Errorf("failed to write key fixture to %s: %v", certFixturePath, err)
                }
        }
```

Typically keys are created with more restrictive modes to prevent other
users or services on the system from being able to copy them before the
user fixes the permissions.

Of course, if a more restrictive umask is in place, this may not be a big
deal, but it's an extremely fickle piece of the environment to rely upon
for security. It's better to be explicit.

Thanks
